        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Treadmills Do Gyms Use? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Most US gyms use commercial treadmills from a small group of makers — chiefly Life
            Fitness, Precor, Matrix, Technogym and Star Trac, with Cybex, True, Woodway, Freemotion and
            Landice also common in some kinds of facility. Which one your gym has depends on the club,
            the franchise owner and when it last re-equipped, so the reliable way to know is to read the
            badge on the machine.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            What these treadmills have in common matters more than the logo. They are built for many
            users and many hours a day: typically an AC drive motor, a heavy steel frame, a thick
            deck, a high user-weight rating and a dedicated electrical circuit, sold with commercial
            warranty and service terms. That is why a gym treadmill feels steadier than most home
            machines, and why putting one in a house is a bigger job than it looks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Claims online about which brand a particular chain uses — Planet Fitness, LA Fitness, 24
            Hour Fitness and the rest — mostly come from sites that disagree with each other. We found
            no reliable current source for any big chain's treadmill brand. Planet Fitness's own
            annual report says its franchisees must buy Planet Fitness-branded equipment from the
            company, which sources it from third-party manufacturers it does not name there. The
            sections below cover each maker, what makes their machines different, and how to check
            yours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-makers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Commercial Treadmill Makers You Will Meet in US Gyms</h2>
<p>
            Commercial fitness is a concentrated business. A handful of companies supply most of the
            cardio in American health clubs, hotels, universities, apartment buildings and corporate
            gyms, and several of the familiar names now belong to larger groups. Here is who owns what,
            as of October 2026.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Brand</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Owner or parent</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Treadmills you may see</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Life Fitness</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">KPS Capital Partners (private equity) since 2019</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Integrity series, Platinum Club Series</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Precor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Peloton since April 2021</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRM series</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Matrix</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Johnson Health Tech</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T-series club treadmills</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Technogym</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Technogym S.p.A., Cesena, Italy; listed in Milan</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Club and hotel treadmills</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Star Trac</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Core Health &amp; Fitness</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4TR, 6TR, 8TRx, FreeRunner 10TRx</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cybex</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A Life Fitness brand</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Mostly older club units</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRUE Fitness</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRUE Fitness, St. Charles, Missouri</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial treadmills</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Woodway</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Woodway, Waukesha, Wisconsin</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slat-belt treadmills such as the 4Front; the manual Curve</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Freemotion</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT, the company behind NordicTrack and ProForm</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REFLEX treadmill and other connected cardio</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Landice</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Family-run, New Jersey</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">L-Series club and rehabilitation models</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Life Fitness</h3>
          <p>
            Life Fitness is one of the best-known names in gym cardio. Brunswick Corporation
            sold its fitness business, including Life Fitness, Hammer Strength and Cybex, to the private
            equity firm KPS Capital Partners for about $490 million in 2019. Life Fitness today lists
            Life Fitness, Hammer Strength, Cybex and SCIFIT as its brands. Its Integrity treadmill is a
            typical mainstream club machine: Life Fitness lists a 4 HP AC motor, a 22 by 60-inch
            running surface, a 400 lb maximum user weight, speeds of 0.5 to 14 mph, incline to 15% and
            a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit. Our
            <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness brand guide</a>
            and <a href="/life-fitness-integrity-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Integrity Series review</a>
            cover the range.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Precor</h3>
          <p>
            Precor, long a fixture of club and hotel gyms, has been owned by Peloton since 1 April
            2021, when Peloton completed a $420 million purchase from Amer Sports. In 2025 Peloton
            combined Precor with its Peloton for Business unit into a Commercial Business Unit, and in
            2026 it announced a Peloton Commercial Series of gym bike and treadmill, with shipments due
            to begin in late 2026. Precor's own treadmills continue: its brochure for the TRM 885 gives
            a 4 HP AC motor and a 120-volt, 20-amp individual branch circuit for the US, and retailer
            listings agree on the motor. Our <a href="/precor-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor treadmill guide</a> covers the current TRM lines and used prices. Listings disagree on the TRM 885's running-surface width and
            maximum user weight, so we leave those out.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Matrix</h3>
          <p>
            Matrix is the commercial brand of Johnson Health Tech, the Taiwan-based group that also
            makes Horizon and Vision home treadmills. Matrix supplies clubs, studios and hotels, and it
            also sells a retail line for homes. Its T1 and T3 series club treadmills were the subject
            of a CPSC recall in 2022, expanded in 2024, because the power cord can work loose and pose
            a fire hazard; the remedy is a power cord bracket. In January 2026 the recall was extended
            to some Matrix retail and Vision home treadmills. Our
            <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix Fitness guide</a>
            covers the brand's home models.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Technogym</h3>
          <p>
            Technogym is an Italian company founded in 1983 by Nerio Alessandri and based in Cesena,
            near Bologna. It has been listed on the Milan stock exchange since 2016. It positions
            itself at the premium end of the market, and its treadmills are a common sight in upscale
            clubs and hotel gyms. It sells to homes too, at premium prices; our
            <a href="/technogym-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Technogym treadmill guide</a> covers the US home range.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Star Trac</h3>
          <p>
            Star Trac belongs to Core Health &amp; Fitness, which also owns <a href="/stairmaster-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>StairMaster</a> and the
            commercial Schwinn and Nautilus lines. Its current treadmills run from the 4TR, aimed at
            hotels and apartment-building gyms, through the 6TR and the 8TRx to the FreeRunner 10TRx.
            Star Trac treadmills are very common on the used market because clubs replace them on a
            cycle; our <a href="/star-trac-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Star Trac guide</a>
            covers what to check on one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cybex, TRUE, Woodway, Freemotion and Landice</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Cybex</strong> is now part of Life Fitness, and Cybex treadmills turn up mostly as
            older units in clubs and on the used market. Older Cybex treadmills were recalled in 2003,
            2007 and 2008, so check any used one against the recall database.
            <strong>TRUE Fitness</strong> is an American maker in the St. Louis area — its site now gives St. Charles, Missouri, where older notices said O'Fallon; our <a href="/true-fitness-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">TRUE treadmill guide</a> covers its home range and warranty.
            <strong>Woodway</strong> (<a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our Woodway guide</a>), based in Waukesha, Wisconsin, builds treadmills with a running
            surface of vulcanised rubber slats instead of a conventional belt — the company says it can
            last more than 150,000 miles — and its machines are often found in performance-training
            facilities; its Curve is a manual curved treadmill, a category covered in our
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>.
            <strong>Freemotion</strong> is the commercial equipment brand of iFIT, pairing gym hardware
            with iFIT's workout content. <strong>Landice</strong>, a small family-run New Jersey maker,
            sells club and rehabilitation versions of its L-Series, which is why you may meet one in a
            physical therapy clinic or a university gym; our
            <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Landice guide</a>
            covers the range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="big-chains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Treadmills Do Planet Fitness, LA Fitness and Other Chains Use?</h2>
<p>
            This is the question most people mean, and the honest answer is that it is not reliably
            published — and that it would not be one answer if it were.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Planet Fitness</strong> is the clearest case, because as a public company it
            describes its equipment arrangements in its annual report. The report for 2025 says
            franchisees are required to buy their fitness equipment from Planet Fitness, describes it as
            "Planet Fitness branded" equipment, says the company buys it from third-party manufacturers, and that franchisees generally replace it every five to nine
            years depending on club volume. It does not name the manufacturers in the sections we read.
            Websites that do name them contradict each other: we found pages saying the chain uses
            Matrix, others saying Star Trac, and others saying Precor. None cited a source we could
            check, so we are not repeating any of them as fact. Because the equipment is club-branded
            and replaced on a cycle, two Planet Fitness clubs — or the same club five years apart — may
            well have different makers' machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>LA Fitness, 24 Hour Fitness, Crunch, Anytime Fitness, Gold's Gym and the YMCA</strong>
            have no current, public equipment disclosure we could find for their US treadmills. Many
            of these are franchised or independently run at club level, buy through regional dealers,
            and refresh on their own schedules. Trade press over the years has reported particular
            supply deals, but an old supply announcement tells you little about the machine in your
            club today.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mixed floors are normal.</strong> A club may run one maker's treadmills next to
            another maker's ellipticals and a third maker's stair climbers, or keep older treadmills
            from one brand alongside new ones from another after a partial refresh. Hotels, apartment
            buildings and offices tend to buy whatever their dealer and budget point to. The only answer
            that is reliably right is the one printed on the machine, which the next section shows you
            how to find.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="find-yours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How to Find Out What Treadmill Your Gym Uses</h2>
<p>
            It takes about a minute, and you can do it before or after a workout without asking anyone.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Read the console and the motor hood.</strong> The maker's name is usually printed on the console, on the plastic cover over the motor at the front of the deck, or along the side rails. On club-branded equipment the club's logo may be the most prominent, so look for the smaller maker's name too.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the model name on the console.</strong> Names such as Integrity, TRM, 8TRx or T-series identify the line; the console may also be a separate model from the base.</li>
            <li><strong>Find the serial label.</strong> Commercial treadmills carry a label with the model and serial number, commonly low on the front of the frame near the <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>power switch</a> or socket — Matrix, for example, places it on the lower front crossbar. Photograph it; it gives the exact model and often the year of manufacture.</li>
            <li><strong>Search the model and the word "specifications".</strong> The maker's spec sheet tells you the motor, deck size and electrical requirement. If you want one at home, that last line matters most.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask the staff.</strong> Front-desk staff may not know, but whoever handles maintenance will, and they can usually tell you how old the machines are and when they are due to be replaced.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are trying to match your gym's machine at home, write down the deck size and the
            incline and speed range as well as the brand. Those are what made it feel the way it does,
            and they are what you can find on a home machine for far less than a commercial one costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-makes-commercial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What Makes a Gym Treadmill Different From a Home Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            A gym treadmill lives a different life. It may run for most of the day, every day, with
            users of every weight and stride, back to back with no cooling time. Every difference
            follows from that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>AC drive motors.</strong> Most commercial treadmills use AC induction motors,
            which run cool under sustained load and have no brushes to wear out. Most home treadmills
            use DC motors, which are quieter, more efficient on household power and cheaper to build.
            Both Life Fitness and Precor list 4 HP AC motors on their mainstream club models. Our
            <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AC vs DC motor guide</a>
            explains why each suits its setting.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Duty ratings.</strong> Commercial machines are designed and warrantied by use
            class — full commercial for clubs, light commercial for hotels, apartment buildings and
            offices, and residential for homes. The warranty terms, not just the hardware, change with
            the class, and using a residential machine in a commercial setting typically voids its
            warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frames and weight.</strong> Club treadmills are heavy, non-folding steel structures.
            Life Fitness lists its Integrity at 425 to 434 pounds, against roughly 200 to 300 pounds for a
            typical full-size home machine. The mass is much of why a gym treadmill does not shift or
            flex under a hard runner.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Decks and rollers.</strong> A running surface of 22 by 60 inches is the club
            norm, wider and longer than many home decks, with thicker decks, larger rollers and
            cushioning systems tuned for thousands of different users. A 400 lb user-weight rating is
            common.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Electrical supply.</strong> Commercial treadmills need a dedicated circuit. Both
            the Integrity and the TRM 885 call for a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit in the US, which
            usually means a 20-amp outlet whose plug will not fit an ordinary 15-amp receptacle. Some
            commercial equipment needs 208 or 240 volts instead, so the "gym treadmills all need 220"
            idea is half true: some do, and many do not. Read the installation manual for the exact
            model.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Service, not just warranty.</strong> Gyms buy treadmills with service contracts
            and preventive maintenance schedules — belt and deck checks, cleaning and calibration — because
            a machine out of service costs them members. Warranties are written around that
            arrangement, and service networks are built around commercial sites, which is one reason
            an ex-gym machine can be hard to get serviced at a private address.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price.</strong> Club treadmills are sold by quote. Our
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial-grade treadmill roundup</a>
            and <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills over $2,500</a>
            cover what that money buys in a home.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4"></th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Life Fitness Integrity</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Precor TRM 885</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 HP AC (8 HP peak)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 HP AC</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running surface</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 × 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sources disagree</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Maximum user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sources disagree</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">US electrical</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dedicated 120 V, 20 A</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">120 V, 20 A individual branch circuit</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Machine weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">425–434 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not confirmed</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Figures are those the maker publishes and at least one independent retailer agrees with;
            where they conflict we say so rather than choose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-gyms-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Why Gyms Pick the Brands They Do</h2>
<p>
            A gym does not buy a treadmill the way you do. It buys a fleet, a service relationship and
            a ten-year cost, and the brand that wins is often the one that wins on those terms rather
            than on the running feel.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Total cost of ownership.</strong> Purchase or lease price, service cost, parts availability and downtime over the machine's working life matter more to an operator than the sticker on any single unit.</li>
            <li><strong>The service network.</strong> A club needs a technician within reach quickly. Brands with dense dealer and service coverage in a region have an advantage there.</li>
            <li><strong>Package deals.</strong> Several of these groups sell strength equipment as well as cardio — Life Fitness with Hammer Strength, Core with Nautilus and StairMaster — so a club fitting out a whole floor may buy most of it from one supplier.</li>
            <li><strong>Consoles and content.</strong> Entertainment screens, member app connections and workout content are part of the pitch, and some chains specify a console that carries their own branding.</li>
            <li><strong>Financing and refresh cycles.</strong> Clubs often lease or finance equipment and replace it on a schedule; Planet Fitness's franchisees, for example, generally replace theirs every five to nine years.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of that tells you which treadmill is best for your home. The best gym brand for a
            200-unit chain is the one with the right service contract; the best treadmill for your
            spare room is a different question, and usually a much cheaper answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="at-home" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Can You Buy the Same Treadmill Your Gym Uses?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, in four ways, and they differ a great deal in cost and difficulty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>New, from a commercial dealer.</strong> Most commercial makers will sell to a
            private buyer through a dealer, on a quote, with freight delivery. Expect commercial
            pricing, and check whether the warranty covers residential use of a commercial machine;
            the terms are not always the same.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Remanufactured or used ex-gym.</strong> This is how most home buyers end up with a
            club treadmill, because gyms retire them on a cycle. A remanufactured unit from a
            specialist reseller with a written warranty is the safer route; a private or auction
            purchase is cheaper and riskier. Our guide to
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-cheap-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a cheap treadmill</a>
            covers surplus auctions and closeouts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A commercial maker's home line.</strong> Life Fitness, Matrix, Technogym and
            Landice all sell machines aimed at homes, often sharing parts or design with their club
            models. They are not identical to the gym machine, so compare the specification sheet
            rather than the badge.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A heavy-duty home treadmill.</strong> A well-built home machine with a 60-inch deck
            and a strong continuous motor reproduces most of what you like about the gym treadmill, on
            household power, for much less.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before any of the first three, read our guide to putting a
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym treadmill in your home</a>,
            which covers floor loading, stairs, ceiling height and the dedicated circuit. And if the
            real question is whether to keep paying for the gym at all, our
            <a href="/home-treadmill-vs-gym-membership/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home treadmill vs gym membership</a>
            comparison works through the arithmetic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="feel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Why Your Home Treadmill Feels Different From the Gym’s</h2>
<p>
            Many people who move from a gym treadmill to a home one notice that the same pace feels
            different. Several real differences add up.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Mass and stiffness.</strong> A 400-pound frame barely moves under a runner. A lighter folding machine can flex and bounce slightly, which changes how each footstrike feels.</li>
            <li><strong>Motor response.</strong> A motor working well within its capacity holds belt speed steadily as your foot lands. One working near its limit can surge and lag slightly with each stride.</li>
            <li><strong>Deck size.</strong> A narrower or shorter belt makes you shorten your stride or run more cautiously, especially at speed.</li>
            <li><strong>Cushioning.</strong> Commercial and home machines tune their decks differently; neither is "right", but the change is noticeable.</li>
            <li><strong>Calibration.</strong> Neither machine's speed display is guaranteed to be exact, and a gym machine on a regular service schedule may be calibrated more often than a home one.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical fix is to shop for the specifications that made the gym machine feel right —
            deck length and width, a continuous motor rating with headroom, and a solid, non-folding
            or heavy folding frame. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            show what to look for.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What brand of treadmill does Planet Fitness use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Planet Fitness does not publish a single treadmill brand. Its annual report says franchisees must buy Planet Fitness-branded equipment from the company, which sources it from third-party manufacturers, and replace it every five to nine years. Online claims naming one maker contradict each other, so read the maker's name and model on the machine at your club.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What treadmills does LA Fitness use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We found no reliable, current public source naming the treadmill brand LA Fitness uses in its US clubs. Equipment varies by club and changes when a club re-equips. Look for the maker's name on the console or motor hood and the model on the serial label near the power switch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most common treadmill brand in gyms?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No published figure ranks gym treadmill brands by number installed. The makers most often found in US gyms are Life Fitness, Precor, Matrix, Technogym and Star Trac, with Cybex, TRUE, Woodway, Freemotion and Landice common in some kinds of facility.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are gym treadmills better than home treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are built for heavier use: usually an AC motor that runs cool under sustained load, a heavy steel frame, a thick deck, a 22 by 60-inch running surface, a high user-weight rating and a commercial service contract. That makes them steadier and more durable, but also heavier, far more expensive and dependent on a dedicated electrical circuit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I buy the same treadmill my gym uses?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. You can buy one new through a commercial dealer on a quote, buy a remanufactured or used ex-gym unit, or choose a commercial maker's home line. Before you do, check that your floor, stairs and wiring can take it, since many club treadmills weigh around 400 pounds and need a dedicated 20-amp circuit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do gym treadmills need a 220-volt outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some do and many do not. Life Fitness specifies a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit for its Integrity treadmill, and Precor's brochure gives the same for the TRM 885 in the US. Other commercial equipment needs 208 or 240 volts. Check the installation manual for the exact model before buying one for home.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes About Gym Treadmills</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of the confusion around gym treadmills comes from four assumptions, each easy to check.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a chain uses one brand everywhere</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Equipment varies by club, franchise owner and refresh date, and floors often mix makers. A website naming the brand a chain uses is not evidence; the label on the machine at your club is.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an ex-gym treadmill before checking the power</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Commercial treadmills need a dedicated circuit, often 120 volts at 20 amps and sometimes 208 or 240 volts, with a plug that may not fit a household outlet. Find the requirement for the exact model and get an electrician's quote before you buy.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a brand's home model as its gym model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Commercial makers sell home lines that share a name and some design with their club machines, but not necessarily the motor, frame or deck. Compare the specification sheets, not the badge.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring recalls on retired club machines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Ex-gym treadmills stay in service for years after a recall. Matrix T1 and T3 club treadmills, for example, need a power cord bracket under a CPSC recall. Check the model and serial number before you buy one used.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

